1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,907
You wanted to be a shield-maiden.

2
00:00:01,907 --> 00:00:02,916
A painted shield,

3
00:00:02,916 --> 00:00:06,160
a sword, your braids flying as you charge into battle.

4
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:07,768
Instead, it is barely light,

5
00:00:07,768 --> 00:00:10,484
your fingers are numb, and you are watching your

6
00:00:10,484 --> 00:00:13,440
husband's ship row out of the fjord without you.

7
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:15,485
Somebody has to stay behind.

8
00:00:15,485 --> 00:00:17,225
Somebody always does.

9
00:00:17,225 --> 00:00:19,945
This summer, that somebody is you.

10
00:00:19,945 --> 00:00:21,705
Imagine you are Aslaug,

11
00:00:21,705 --> 00:00:24,345
the wife of Eirik from our last two chapters,

12
00:00:24,345 --> 00:00:25,678
on a farm on the western

13
00:00:25,678 --> 00:00:28,105
coast of Norway in the tenth century.

14
00:00:28,105 --> 00:00:30,608
Like Eirik, you are an imagined person,

15
00:00:30,608 --> 00:00:32,630
built from what archaeology,

16
00:00:32,630 --> 00:00:34,836
laws and written sources tell us,

17
00:00:34,836 --> 00:00:37,270
not a real woman from history.

18
00:00:37,270 --> 00:00:38,453
Eirik is rowing for Bjorn

19
00:00:38,453 --> 00:00:40,071
on a raid across the sea.

20
00:00:40,071 --> 00:00:41,254
You have heard the

21
00:00:41,254 --> 00:00:42,873
songs about men like him.

22
00:00:42,873 --> 00:00:44,491
You have also noticed who

23
00:00:44,491 --> 00:00:46,110
stays home with the cows.

24
00:00:46,110 --> 00:00:47,728
Popular culture loves the

25
00:00:47,728 --> 00:00:49,409
Viking woman with a sword.

26
00:00:49,409 --> 00:00:51,713
And to be fair, Norse women had some

27
00:00:51,713 --> 00:00:54,665
rights that were unusual for their time.

28
00:00:54,665 --> 00:00:55,883
We will get to those.

29
00:00:55,883 --> 00:00:58,830
But your day does not start with a battle.

30
00:00:58,830 --> 00:01:00,750
It starts with a list.

31
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:02,270
Milk the cows.

32
00:01:02,270 --> 00:01:03,950
Feed the household.

33
00:01:03,950 --> 00:01:05,470
Check the grain.

34
00:01:05,470 --> 00:01:06,510
Spin.

35
00:01:06,510 --> 00:01:07,710
Weave.

36
00:01:07,710 --> 00:01:09,470
Watch the sky.

37
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:10,490
Spin again.

38
00:01:10,925 --> 00:01:12,755
Somewhere on that list there is

39
00:01:12,755 --> 00:01:15,029
supposed to be time for sword practice.

40
00:01:15,029 --> 00:01:16,125
There is not.

41
00:01:16,125 --> 00:01:17,891
And at the end of this story,

42
00:01:17,891 --> 00:01:20,598
we will open a real grave in Sweden that made

43
00:01:20,598 --> 00:01:22,187
headlines around the world,

44
00:01:22,187 --> 00:01:25,837
and ask what scholars actually concluded about warrior women.

45
00:01:25,837 --> 00:01:27,720
The moment the sail disappears,

46
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:29,320
the farm is yours.

47
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:32,113
The National Museum of Denmark puts it simply:

48
00:01:32,113 --> 00:01:34,600
when a man was called on an expedition,

49
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:35,734
responsibility for

50
00:01:35,734 --> 00:01:38,208
the farm was handed over to the woman.

51
00:01:38,208 --> 00:01:39,480
Not the glory.

52
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:41,063
The responsibility.

53
00:01:41,063 --> 00:01:41,990
The animals,

54
00:01:41,990 --> 00:01:44,701
the stores, the fields, the repairs.

55
00:01:44,701 --> 00:01:47,795
If the roof leaks, it is your roof now.

56
00:01:47,795 --> 00:01:48,732
To be honest,

57
00:01:48,732 --> 00:01:51,074
it has been your roof for a while.

58
00:01:51,074 --> 00:01:53,751
Old poetry gives you a symbol for this.

59
00:01:53,751 --> 00:01:55,882
A poem called Rígsþula describes

60
00:01:55,882 --> 00:01:57,836
a farmer's bride arriving at her

61
00:01:57,836 --> 00:02:00,026
new home with keys hanging from her.

62
00:02:00,026 --> 00:02:02,098
For a long time, keys were read as

63
00:02:02,098 --> 00:02:04,410
the badge of the Viking housewife.

64
00:02:04,410 --> 00:02:06,109
The graves complicate that.

65
00:02:06,109 --> 00:02:07,990
Keys turn up in only about one

66
00:02:07,990 --> 00:02:09,325
woman's grave in twenty,

67
00:02:09,325 --> 00:02:12,295
according to the National Museum of Denmark.

68
00:02:12,295 --> 00:02:14,935
And in Vestfold, in southern Norway,

69
00:02:14,935 --> 00:02:17,415
the archaeologist Marianne Moen found keys

70
00:02:17,415 --> 00:02:20,135
in almost as many men's graves as women's.

71
00:02:20,135 --> 00:02:22,610
Some, like the Swedish History Museum,

72
00:02:22,610 --> 00:02:23,911
still see the key as a

73
00:02:23,911 --> 00:02:26,290
sign of authority over the household.

74
00:02:26,290 --> 00:02:27,544
Others suspect it meant

75
00:02:27,544 --> 00:02:29,170
something else entirely.

76
00:02:29,170 --> 00:02:31,563
The archaeologist Pernille Pantmann has

77
00:02:31,563 --> 00:02:34,021
suggested that keys marked a special

78
00:02:34,021 --> 00:02:35,702
role held by only a few,

79
00:02:35,702 --> 00:02:39,845
perhaps a wise woman, someone with knowledge others lacked.

80
00:02:39,845 --> 00:02:40,642
Either way,

81
00:02:40,642 --> 00:02:43,765
your authority does not come from a key.

82
00:02:43,765 --> 00:02:45,275
It comes from the fact that if

83
00:02:45,275 --> 00:02:47,425
you stop working, everyone eats less.

84
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:50,154
Scholars broadly agree on this much:

85
00:02:50,154 --> 00:02:53,510
a woman's power was strongest inside the house.

86
00:02:53,510 --> 00:02:55,910
Out in public, men ran things.

87
00:02:55,910 --> 00:02:57,860
You find that out when your neighbor

88
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:00,710
returns to his favorite subject: the boundary.

89
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:01,940
With Eirik away,

90
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:05,247
he suggests that the marker stone may have,

91
00:03:05,247 --> 00:03:08,105
somehow, wandered onto your land.

92
00:03:08,105 --> 00:03:10,505
Can you take him to the assembly yourself?

93
00:03:10,505 --> 00:03:11,425
Probably not.

94
00:03:11,425 --> 00:03:12,871
In Iceland's law book,

95
00:03:12,871 --> 00:03:15,895
written down later, a woman could not bring a

96
00:03:15,895 --> 00:03:18,830
case unless a man prosecuted it for her.

97
00:03:18,830 --> 00:03:20,735
The National Museum of Denmark

98
00:03:20,735 --> 00:03:23,390
says women could not appear in court.

99
00:03:23,390 --> 00:03:25,710
Rules varied from place to place,

100
00:03:25,710 --> 00:03:27,364
and historians still debate how

101
00:03:27,364 --> 00:03:29,370
strictly they applied in Norway.

102
00:03:29,675 --> 00:03:31,246
But for a married woman,

103
00:03:31,246 --> 00:03:34,315
the usual road to justice ran through a man.

104
00:03:34,315 --> 00:03:36,795
So the boundary waits for Eirik.

105
00:03:36,795 --> 00:03:37,595
Your neighbor,

106
00:03:37,595 --> 00:03:39,435
meanwhile, waits for nothing.

107
00:03:39,435 --> 00:03:40,409
While you wait,

108
00:03:40,409 --> 00:03:42,357
think back to how you got here.

109
00:03:42,357 --> 00:03:43,392
You did not meet

110
00:03:43,392 --> 00:03:45,335
Eirik on a cliff at sunset.

111
00:03:45,650 --> 00:03:48,290
Your marriage was arranged between families,

112
00:03:48,290 --> 00:03:50,770
and it was very much about property.

113
00:03:50,770 --> 00:03:52,770
Eirik paid a bride-price,

114
00:03:52,770 --> 00:03:56,210
a gift that in the law became your own property.

115
00:03:56,210 --> 00:03:58,450
Your father provided a dowry.

116
00:03:58,450 --> 00:04:00,405
Both families counted carefully.

117
00:04:00,405 --> 00:04:02,325
Romance was optional.

118
00:04:02,325 --> 00:04:03,774
The paperwork was not,

119
00:04:03,774 --> 00:04:05,605
even without any paper.

120
00:04:05,605 --> 00:04:07,605
Who did the negotiating?

121
00:04:07,605 --> 00:04:09,925
Fathers and male relatives.

122
00:04:09,925 --> 00:04:12,900
The bride was often asked, but not always.

123
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:15,001
As the scholar Else Mundal sums

124
00:04:15,001 --> 00:04:17,380
up the laws of Norway and Iceland,

125
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:19,884
a woman could not choose the man she married,

126
00:04:19,884 --> 00:04:21,460
unless she was a widow.

127
00:04:21,460 --> 00:04:24,130
Which leads to an uncomfortable conclusion:

128
00:04:24,130 --> 00:04:25,663
the woman with the most say

129
00:04:25,663 --> 00:04:27,524
over her own marriage was usually

130
00:04:27,524 --> 00:04:29,845
one who had already buried a husband.

131
00:04:29,845 --> 00:04:31,685
A saga tells of Gudrun,

132
00:04:31,685 --> 00:04:34,965
an Icelandic woman promised in marriage at fifteen.

133
00:04:34,965 --> 00:04:37,045
Her father settled the contract.

134
00:04:37,045 --> 00:04:39,361
The saga says plainly that she was not

135
00:04:39,361 --> 00:04:41,975
asked about it, and that she was against it.

136
00:04:41,975 --> 00:04:43,281
But here is where the

137
00:04:43,281 --> 00:04:45,182
Viking world surprises people.

138
00:04:45,182 --> 00:04:46,192
You could leave.

139
00:04:46,192 --> 00:04:48,033
A wife could seek a divorce by

140
00:04:48,033 --> 00:04:50,290
gathering witnesses and declaring her

141
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:53,600
complaint and her intention to end the marriage.

142
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:55,955
And she could claim her dowry back.

143
00:04:55,955 --> 00:04:57,438
Gudrun used that right.

144
00:04:57,438 --> 00:04:58,241
In the saga,

145
00:04:58,241 --> 00:05:01,075
her husband slaps her during an argument.

146
00:05:01,075 --> 00:05:02,985
A friend advises her to make him a

147
00:05:02,985 --> 00:05:04,841
shirt with a neckline so low that

148
00:05:04,841 --> 00:05:07,075
it will give her grounds for divorce.

149
00:05:07,075 --> 00:05:07,845
That spring,

150
00:05:07,845 --> 00:05:10,274
she announces the divorce and goes home.

151
00:05:10,274 --> 00:05:11,932
When the estate is divided,

152
00:05:11,932 --> 00:05:14,740
she leaves with half of all the property.

153
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:15,772
In the same saga,

154
00:05:15,772 --> 00:05:18,526
a man divorces his wife on the grounds that she

155
00:05:18,526 --> 00:05:21,325
made herself breeches cut like a man's.

156
00:05:21,325 --> 00:05:22,473
In the saga world,

157
00:05:22,473 --> 00:05:24,952
the wrong clothes could end a marriage.

158
00:05:24,952 --> 00:05:27,005
Choose your sewing carefully.

159
00:05:27,005 --> 00:05:28,518
The right itself was real

160
00:05:28,518 --> 00:05:30,365
enough to impress outsiders.

161
00:05:30,365 --> 00:05:32,948
A traveller from Muslim Spain who visited

162
00:05:32,948 --> 00:05:35,202
the trading town of Hedeby in the nine

163
00:05:35,202 --> 00:05:37,557
hundreds is reported to have said that women

164
00:05:37,557 --> 00:05:40,080
there could divorce whenever they wished.

165
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:42,960
Some historians suspect he exaggerated.

166
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:45,190
Still, by the standards of the time,

167
00:05:45,190 --> 00:05:47,900
a wife's right to leave was remarkable.

168
00:05:48,215 --> 00:05:49,510
Before you celebrate,

169
00:05:49,510 --> 00:05:51,655
read the rest of the contract.

170
00:05:51,655 --> 00:05:53,324
Under the laws of both

171
00:05:53,324 --> 00:05:54,775
Norway and Iceland,

172
00:05:54,775 --> 00:05:57,655
a daughter inherited half as much as a son.

173
00:05:57,655 --> 00:05:59,655
So yes, you have rights.

174
00:05:59,655 --> 00:06:02,267
Some of them were unusual for the age.

175
00:06:02,267 --> 00:06:04,744
They just come with a smaller share,

176
00:06:04,744 --> 00:06:06,820
a man's voice at the assembly,

177
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:08,900
and a great deal of spinning.

178
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:10,195
Which brings us to the

179
00:06:10,195 --> 00:06:12,180
real engine of the Viking Age.

180
00:06:12,180 --> 00:06:13,700
It is not a sword.

181
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:15,920
It is a stick with a weight on the end.

182
00:06:16,435 --> 00:06:18,195
Every thread on this farm

183
00:06:18,195 --> 00:06:20,195
starts as a tuft of wool.

184
00:06:20,195 --> 00:06:22,021
You clean it and prepare it,

185
00:06:22,021 --> 00:06:24,115
then spin it on a drop spindle,

186
00:06:24,115 --> 00:06:26,545
a weighted stick twirling below your hand.

187
00:06:26,545 --> 00:06:28,692
Then you weave it on an upright loom,

188
00:06:28,692 --> 00:06:31,760
its threads pulled tight by hanging weights.

189
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:34,240
You spin while you watch the porridge,

190
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:35,270
while you talk,

191
00:06:35,270 --> 00:06:37,331
while you walk to the cowshed.

192
00:06:37,331 --> 00:06:39,920
The spindle goes wherever you go.

193
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:41,375
This was women's work.

194
00:06:41,375 --> 00:06:43,715
The National Museum of Denmark notes

195
00:06:43,715 --> 00:06:46,182
that finds point to textile production

196
00:06:46,182 --> 00:06:47,257
as women's work,

197
00:06:47,257 --> 00:06:50,736
and medieval sources suggest women handled every step,

198
00:06:50,736 --> 00:06:53,965
from gathering the wool to weaving the cloth.

199
00:06:53,965 --> 00:06:56,369
Everyone's clothes come from your hands:

200
00:06:56,369 --> 00:06:57,285
tunics, cloaks,

201
00:06:57,285 --> 00:07:00,905
blankets, and the shirt Eirik is currently ruining at sea.

202
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:03,119
And then there is the sail.

203
00:07:03,119 --> 00:07:04,545
That great square sail

204
00:07:04,545 --> 00:07:07,320
carrying Eirik's ship across the sea?

205
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:09,331
Someone made it, thread by thread.

206
00:07:09,331 --> 00:07:10,710
How long did that take?

207
00:07:10,710 --> 00:07:12,089
Nobody kept a timesheet,

208
00:07:12,089 --> 00:07:13,940
so we only have estimates.

209
00:07:14,375 --> 00:07:16,174
Researchers at the Viking Ship

210
00:07:16,174 --> 00:07:17,415
Museum in Roskilde,

211
00:07:17,415 --> 00:07:20,615
working with Viking-style spindles and looms,

212
00:07:20,615 --> 00:07:23,136
estimated that five people could spin and

213
00:07:23,136 --> 00:07:24,577
weave the cloth for one

214
00:07:24,577 --> 00:07:27,399
reconstructed cargo ship's sail in about a year.

215
00:07:27,399 --> 00:07:29,920
The archaeologist Lise Bender Jørgensen,

216
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:33,040
who studies ancient textiles, went further.

217
00:07:33,040 --> 00:07:34,832
Counting the sail and the other

218
00:07:34,832 --> 00:07:36,568
textiles a crew needed at sea,

219
00:07:36,568 --> 00:07:40,245
she estimated around ten years of work for a cargo ship.

220
00:07:40,245 --> 00:07:43,445
For a warship with sixty-five to seventy men:

221
00:07:43,445 --> 00:07:45,224
fifty to sixty years of work,

222
00:07:45,224 --> 00:07:47,833
and more than one and a half tonnes of wool.

223
00:07:47,833 --> 00:07:49,968
Those are estimates, and they vary.

224
00:07:49,968 --> 00:07:52,165
But they all point the same way.

225
00:07:52,165 --> 00:07:53,730
Behind every famous sail

226
00:07:53,730 --> 00:07:55,482
stood years of women's work,

227
00:07:55,482 --> 00:07:58,090
and a remarkable number of sheep.

228
00:07:58,090 --> 00:08:01,610
Cloth was so valuable that in medieval Iceland,

229
00:08:01,610 --> 00:08:02,129
a little

230
00:08:02,129 --> 00:08:04,410
after your time, it became money.

231
00:08:04,410 --> 00:08:06,542
The law fixed the length and width of

232
00:08:06,542 --> 00:08:09,095
a standard woollen cloth called vaðmál,

233
00:08:09,095 --> 00:08:11,764
and people paid taxes and fines with it.

234
00:08:11,764 --> 00:08:13,457
The archaeologist Michèle

235
00:08:13,457 --> 00:08:15,895
Hayeur Smith points out the irony.

236
00:08:15,895 --> 00:08:18,318
Women had no say in making those laws,

237
00:08:18,318 --> 00:08:19,809
yet they wove the cloth

238
00:08:19,809 --> 00:08:22,045
that the whole legal system ran on.

239
00:08:22,045 --> 00:08:23,288
Eirik will come home

240
00:08:23,288 --> 00:08:24,716
talking about the sea.

241
00:08:24,716 --> 00:08:26,740
He will not mention the loom.

242
00:08:26,740 --> 00:08:28,857
Nobody ever mentions the loom.

243
00:08:28,857 --> 00:08:30,359
At the end of summer,

244
00:08:30,359 --> 00:08:32,680
he does come home, with a cut arm,

245
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:35,889
a small pouch of silver and a lot of stories.

246
00:08:35,889 --> 00:08:37,733
You look at the arm first.

247
00:08:37,733 --> 00:08:39,145
Then the pouch.

248
00:08:39,145 --> 00:08:41,465
There is no time for a feast.

249
00:08:41,465 --> 00:08:44,105
Autumn is the season of hard choices.

250
00:08:44,105 --> 00:08:45,188
Some animals will be

251
00:08:45,188 --> 00:08:46,745
fed through the winter.

252
00:08:46,745 --> 00:08:48,637
Others must be slaughtered now.

253
00:08:48,637 --> 00:08:51,410
Everything that can be kept, is kept.

254
00:08:51,410 --> 00:08:54,530
Grain, berries and vegetables are dried.

255
00:08:54,530 --> 00:08:57,330
Meat is dried, smoked or salted.

256
00:08:57,330 --> 00:08:59,124
Fish is dried and stacked.

257
00:08:59,124 --> 00:09:01,251
Milk becomes butter and cheese,

258
00:09:01,251 --> 00:09:03,511
precious fat for the long winter.

259
00:09:03,511 --> 00:09:06,495
You count, and then you count again.

260
00:09:06,495 --> 00:09:08,122
The National Museum of Denmark

261
00:09:08,122 --> 00:09:09,748
notes that housekeeping had to

262
00:09:09,748 --> 00:09:11,615
be planned around the seasons.

263
00:09:11,615 --> 00:09:13,134
Winter is when you find out

264
00:09:13,134 --> 00:09:15,135
whether the plan was any good.

265
00:09:15,135 --> 00:09:17,855
Then there is the silver in Eirik's pouch.

266
00:09:17,855 --> 00:09:19,886
You know where some of it came from.

267
00:09:19,886 --> 00:09:20,910
Two captives,

268
00:09:20,910 --> 00:09:23,318
sold at a trading place on the way home.

269
00:09:23,318 --> 00:09:24,727
This is the part of the

270
00:09:24,727 --> 00:09:26,670
Viking story with no heroes.

271
00:09:26,670 --> 00:09:29,550
Free women like you had limited rights.

272
00:09:29,550 --> 00:09:31,588
Enslaved women and men could be

273
00:09:31,588 --> 00:09:33,085
bought and sold,

274
00:09:33,085 --> 00:09:36,325
and the National Museum of Denmark describes them as

275
00:09:36,325 --> 00:09:38,925
people without any personal rights.

276
00:09:38,925 --> 00:09:40,911
On a richer farm down the valley,

277
00:09:40,911 --> 00:09:43,085
you can see what that means.

278
00:09:43,085 --> 00:09:45,833
Enslaved people do the heaviest work:

279
00:09:45,833 --> 00:09:49,122
mending fences, spreading dung on the fields,

280
00:09:49,122 --> 00:09:52,160
herding pigs and sheep, cutting turf.

281
00:09:52,160 --> 00:09:55,296
The poem Rígsþula imagines the family of

282
00:09:55,296 --> 00:09:58,400
a thrall and hands them exactly those jobs.

283
00:09:58,400 --> 00:10:00,275
It is a poem about society,

284
00:10:00,275 --> 00:10:02,355
not a record of real people.

285
00:10:02,355 --> 00:10:04,294
But the list is grimly practical,

286
00:10:04,294 --> 00:10:06,633
and the Swedish History Museum describes

287
00:10:06,633 --> 00:10:09,235
thralls' work in almost the same words.

288
00:10:09,235 --> 00:10:11,061
Most enslaved people left almost

289
00:10:11,061 --> 00:10:12,389
no trace in the ground,

290
00:10:12,389 --> 00:10:15,580
apart from the occasional iron collar or shackle.

291
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:17,500
You do not own anyone.

292
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:19,620
But you live in a world that does,

293
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:20,590
and some of its

294
00:10:20,590 --> 00:10:22,710
silver now sits in your storeroom.

295
00:10:22,710 --> 00:10:23,820
The days shrink.

296
00:10:23,820 --> 00:10:25,340
The hearth smokes.

297
00:10:25,340 --> 00:10:26,680
The stores go down a

298
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:28,722
little faster than you planned.

299
00:10:28,722 --> 00:10:29,615
By midwinter,

300
00:10:29,615 --> 00:10:31,855
you know you are expecting a child.

301
00:10:31,855 --> 00:10:32,262
Here,

302
00:10:32,262 --> 00:10:35,215
that news comes with joy, and with fear.

303
00:10:35,215 --> 00:10:36,678
Childbirth was one of the most

304
00:10:36,678 --> 00:10:39,195
dangerous things a woman in the past could do.

305
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:42,221
Archaeologists studying early

306
00:10:42,221 --> 00:10:44,287
medieval England have called it the

307
00:10:44,287 --> 00:10:46,960
biggest single cause of death for women.

308
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:49,122
The archaeologist Marianne Hem

309
00:10:49,122 --> 00:10:52,333
Eriksen stresses that maternal mortality was

310
00:10:52,333 --> 00:10:55,260
very high in pre-industrial societies.

311
00:10:55,615 --> 00:10:59,535
Strangely, Viking graves barely show it.

312
00:10:59,535 --> 00:11:01,215
Eriksen and her colleague

313
00:11:01,215 --> 00:11:03,206
Katherine Olley went through the

314
00:11:03,206 --> 00:11:05,455
catalogues of Viking Age graves.

315
00:11:05,455 --> 00:11:06,735
Out of thousands,

316
00:11:06,735 --> 00:11:10,473
they found only fourteen where a woman might have been

317
00:11:10,473 --> 00:11:13,350
buried with a newborn or unborn child.

318
00:11:13,350 --> 00:11:15,374
Not because mothers were safe.

319
00:11:15,374 --> 00:11:17,588
Babies are missing from the burial

320
00:11:17,588 --> 00:11:20,580
record in general, and mothers and infants seem

321
00:11:20,580 --> 00:11:23,295
usually not to have been buried together.

322
00:11:23,295 --> 00:11:25,106
Where those babies went,

323
00:11:25,106 --> 00:11:27,535
we still do not fully know.

324
00:11:27,535 --> 00:11:29,775
There is no doctor coming.

325
00:11:29,775 --> 00:11:32,495
There are women who have done this before.

326
00:11:32,495 --> 00:11:34,696
In late spring, a neighbor woman who

327
00:11:34,696 --> 00:11:37,435
has helped at many births comes to stay.

328
00:11:37,790 --> 00:11:38,923
Your labor lasts

329
00:11:38,923 --> 00:11:41,390
through a night and a morning.

330
00:11:41,390 --> 00:11:43,893
Your daughter is born alive.

331
00:11:43,893 --> 00:11:45,310
So are you.

332
00:11:45,310 --> 00:11:48,970
For a moment, that is the whole victory.

333
00:11:49,310 --> 00:11:50,970
Then the worrying begins.

334
00:11:51,295 --> 00:11:53,775
Across pre-modern societies,

335
00:11:53,775 --> 00:11:56,922
researchers estimate that about a quarter of babies

336
00:11:56,922 --> 00:11:58,815
died in their first year,

337
00:11:58,815 --> 00:12:02,575
and around half of all children never reached adulthood.

338
00:12:02,575 --> 00:12:05,653
There is no exact figure for Viking Norway,

339
00:12:05,653 --> 00:12:07,541
but the National Museum of

340
00:12:07,541 --> 00:12:11,091
Denmark is blunt: infant mortality was high.

341
00:12:11,091 --> 00:12:12,989
Runestones carry the same

342
00:12:12,989 --> 00:12:15,280
grief in a few hard words.

343
00:12:15,280 --> 00:12:16,560
Near Stockholm,

344
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:19,511
a runic inscription carved on a rock tells the

345
00:12:19,511 --> 00:12:21,775
story of a woman named Geirlaug.

346
00:12:22,245 --> 00:12:24,037
She was married as a girl.

347
00:12:24,037 --> 00:12:25,845
Her first husband drowned,

348
00:12:25,845 --> 00:12:27,445
and their son died.

349
00:12:27,445 --> 00:12:29,205
With her second husband,

350
00:12:29,205 --> 00:12:33,045
she had children, but only one girl lived: Inga.

351
00:12:33,045 --> 00:12:33,919
Inga married,

352
00:12:33,919 --> 00:12:36,465
lost her husband and then her son.

353
00:12:36,860 --> 00:12:37,852
When Inga died,

354
00:12:37,852 --> 00:12:40,334
her mother Geirlaug inherited from her.

355
00:12:40,334 --> 00:12:43,187
The carving is a family tree written in loss,

356
00:12:43,187 --> 00:12:45,820
and a record of who inherited what.

357
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:48,307
Your own body keeps score as well.

358
00:12:48,307 --> 00:12:50,155
Viking Age skeletons show

359
00:12:50,155 --> 00:12:51,576
arthritis in backs,

360
00:12:51,576 --> 00:12:55,655
hands and knees, the mark of years of heavy work.

361
00:12:55,655 --> 00:12:57,575
Long lives were rare.

362
00:12:57,575 --> 00:12:59,643
A study of adult skeletons from the

363
00:12:59,643 --> 00:13:01,940
Danish town of Ribe found that women of

364
00:13:01,940 --> 00:13:05,133
the Viking period who survived childhood died,

365
00:13:05,133 --> 00:13:07,840
on average, at about thirty-nine.

366
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:10,129
The National Museum of Denmark notes

367
00:13:10,129 --> 00:13:12,800
that only a few people lived past fifty.

368
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:14,628
Early summer brings the merchant

369
00:13:14,628 --> 00:13:15,735
back to the landing,

370
00:13:15,735 --> 00:13:19,085
with his scales and his opinions about your cloth.

371
00:13:19,085 --> 00:13:20,496
You bargain hard.

372
00:13:20,496 --> 00:13:23,405
Trade was not only a man's business.

373
00:13:23,405 --> 00:13:24,605
In Trøndelag,

374
00:13:24,605 --> 00:13:28,736
in central Norway, balance scales, the tool of a trader,

375
00:13:28,736 --> 00:13:30,845
come mostly from men's graves,

376
00:13:30,845 --> 00:13:33,793
but a few women were buried with them too.

377
00:13:33,793 --> 00:13:35,713
The archaeologist Elisabeth

378
00:13:35,713 --> 00:13:38,430
Nordbladh points to women's graves with

379
00:13:38,430 --> 00:13:41,690
scales and weights as far away as Russia.

380
00:13:41,690 --> 00:13:43,610
What did those scales mean?

381
00:13:43,610 --> 00:13:45,830
Perhaps these women traded themselves.

382
00:13:46,295 --> 00:13:49,003
Perhaps the scales belonged to the family,

383
00:13:49,003 --> 00:13:51,015
or meant something else.

384
00:13:51,015 --> 00:13:53,137
Archaeologists are careful here:

385
00:13:53,137 --> 00:13:55,690
an object in a grave does not always

386
00:13:55,690 --> 00:13:58,243
tell you what its owner did in life.

387
00:13:58,243 --> 00:13:59,278
Remember that.

388
00:13:59,278 --> 00:14:01,610
It will matter in a moment.

389
00:14:01,610 --> 00:14:04,090
Some women went very far.

390
00:14:04,090 --> 00:14:05,548
A saga tells of Gudrid,

391
00:14:05,548 --> 00:14:08,281
who sailed west to the land the Norse called

392
00:14:08,281 --> 00:14:10,950
Vinland and gave birth to a son there.

393
00:14:11,315 --> 00:14:14,275
At L'Anse aux Meadows in Newfoundland,

394
00:14:14,275 --> 00:14:16,288
a Norse site from some decades

395
00:14:16,288 --> 00:14:17,392
after your time,

396
00:14:17,392 --> 00:14:21,092
archaeologists found a spindle whorl and a bone needle,

397
00:14:21,092 --> 00:14:23,555
signs that women were there too.

398
00:14:23,555 --> 00:14:26,080
Other women left their mark in stone.

399
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:29,404
Many runestones say that a woman raised them,

400
00:14:29,404 --> 00:14:31,030
a very public act in a

401
00:14:31,030 --> 00:14:34,320
world where women rarely spoke in public.

402
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:35,990
At Hadeland in Norway,

403
00:14:35,990 --> 00:14:39,985
about a century after your time, a mother named Gunnvor

404
00:14:39,985 --> 00:14:43,037
built a bridge in memory of her daughter Astrid,

405
00:14:43,037 --> 00:14:45,345
and raised a stone to say so.

406
00:14:45,345 --> 00:14:47,379
It ends with a mother's boast:

407
00:14:47,379 --> 00:14:50,440
she was the handiest maiden in Hadeland.

408
00:14:50,440 --> 00:14:52,873
And the richest burial of the Viking Age,

409
00:14:52,873 --> 00:14:54,379
according to the National

410
00:14:54,379 --> 00:14:57,560
Museum of Denmark, was made for women.

411
00:14:57,560 --> 00:14:59,720
In eight hundred and thirty-four,

412
00:14:59,720 --> 00:15:03,054
two women, one elderly and one around fifty,

413
00:15:03,054 --> 00:15:04,645
were laid to rest in

414
00:15:04,645 --> 00:15:07,705
the great Oseberg ship in Norway.

415
00:15:07,705 --> 00:15:11,125
So yes, some Norse women owned land,

416
00:15:11,125 --> 00:15:12,025
traded,

417
00:15:12,025 --> 00:15:16,665
travelled, ran households and were honored in stone.

418
00:15:16,665 --> 00:15:18,405
The catch is the word some.

419
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:20,058
Most women's lives

420
00:15:20,058 --> 00:15:21,887
looked a lot more like yours.

421
00:15:21,887 --> 00:15:24,260
Which brings us back to the sword.

422
00:15:24,260 --> 00:15:25,860
In eighteen seventy-eight,

423
00:15:25,860 --> 00:15:28,740
archaeologists excavated a grave at Birka,

424
00:15:28,740 --> 00:15:31,460
a Viking Age trading town in Sweden.

425
00:15:31,460 --> 00:15:33,814
Today it is known as

426
00:15:33,814 --> 00:15:36,725
grave B J five eighty-one.

427
00:15:36,725 --> 00:15:38,269
It held a sword,

428
00:15:38,269 --> 00:15:40,165
an axe, two spears,

429
00:15:40,165 --> 00:15:43,141
armour-piercing arrows, a fighting knife,

430
00:15:43,141 --> 00:15:47,980
two shields, two horses and a full set of gaming pieces.

431
00:15:47,980 --> 00:15:49,451
For more than a century,

432
00:15:49,451 --> 00:15:52,300
the person inside was assumed to be a man.

433
00:15:52,300 --> 00:15:54,031
An earlier study of the bones

434
00:15:54,031 --> 00:15:56,140
had already suggested a woman.

435
00:15:56,140 --> 00:15:57,580
In twenty seventeen,

436
00:15:57,580 --> 00:15:59,931
DNA settled it: the person in the

437
00:15:59,931 --> 00:16:03,085
warrior's grave was biologically female.

438
00:16:03,085 --> 00:16:05,163
Critics first asked whether those

439
00:16:05,163 --> 00:16:07,242
bones really came from that grave,

440
00:16:07,242 --> 00:16:09,885
after more than a century in storage.

441
00:16:09,885 --> 00:16:11,995
The team answered with a detailed

442
00:16:11,995 --> 00:16:14,292
review of the old excavation records,

443
00:16:14,292 --> 00:16:17,130
and published a longer study in twenty nineteen.

444
00:16:17,130 --> 00:16:19,116
The research team concluded that she

445
00:16:19,116 --> 00:16:21,477
most likely lived as a professional warrior

446
00:16:21,477 --> 00:16:23,516
of high rank, while saying openly that

447
00:16:23,516 --> 00:16:26,070
many other interpretations were possible.

448
00:16:26,515 --> 00:16:28,420
Other scholars are less sure.

449
00:16:28,420 --> 00:16:30,642
The historian Judith Jesch pointed

450
00:16:30,642 --> 00:16:32,801
out that weapons in a grave do not

451
00:16:32,801 --> 00:16:34,675
prove that someone fought,

452
00:16:34,675 --> 00:16:37,875
and the skeleton shows no injuries from battle.

453
00:16:37,875 --> 00:16:39,487
Others have suggested that

454
00:16:39,487 --> 00:16:41,099
weapons could be a symbol,

455
00:16:41,099 --> 00:16:44,860
an heirloom, or a statement about family status.

456
00:16:44,860 --> 00:16:47,740
So what do scholars actually conclude?

457
00:16:47,740 --> 00:16:49,386
That one woman at Birka

458
00:16:49,386 --> 00:16:51,340
was buried as a warrior.

459
00:16:51,340 --> 00:16:53,740
Whether she fought is still debated.

460
00:16:53,740 --> 00:16:56,128
And real evidence for women like her,

461
00:16:56,128 --> 00:16:58,327
as the research team itself wrote,

462
00:16:58,327 --> 00:16:59,715
has been sparse.

463
00:16:59,715 --> 00:17:01,564
So why was being a Viking

464
00:17:01,564 --> 00:17:03,955
woman worse than you think?

465
00:17:03,955 --> 00:17:06,435
Because the shield-maiden was rare,

466
00:17:06,435 --> 00:17:08,995
and the loom was everywhere.

467
00:17:08,995 --> 00:17:11,315
You ran the farm without the title,

468
00:17:11,315 --> 00:17:13,519
held rights with a smaller share,

469
00:17:13,519 --> 00:17:15,852
and faced the most dangerous day of

470
00:17:15,852 --> 00:17:18,830
your life with no song written about it.

471
00:17:18,830 --> 00:17:21,950
And yet, it was not all darkness.

472
00:17:21,950 --> 00:17:23,470
You could own property,

473
00:17:23,470 --> 00:17:25,810
leave a bad marriage, raise a stone,

474
00:17:25,810 --> 00:17:29,245
and run the household that kept everyone alive.

475
00:17:29,245 --> 00:17:31,085
Many women elsewhere in Europe

476
00:17:31,085 --> 00:17:33,245
at the time had fewer choices.

477
00:17:33,245 --> 00:17:34,101
Next spring,

478
00:17:34,101 --> 00:17:36,845
Bjorn will talk about another voyage.

479
00:17:36,845 --> 00:17:39,165
Eirik will pretend not to listen.

480
00:17:39,165 --> 00:17:40,755
You will check the stores,

481
00:17:40,755 --> 00:17:43,270
the cradle and the loom, in that order.

482
00:17:43,270 --> 00:17:45,117
And somewhere behind the house,

483
00:17:45,117 --> 00:17:47,110
the sheep has escaped again.

484
00:17:47,110 --> 00:17:49,079
This is Viking Chapters:

485
00:17:49,079 --> 00:17:51,590
the lives behind the legends.

486
00:17:51,590 --> 00:17:53,524
If you would like to see another

487
00:17:53,524 --> 00:17:54,988
life behind the legends,

488
00:17:54,988 --> 00:17:57,713
subscribe, and we will meet in the next chapter.
